1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,800
Meet Rosa.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,699
She's the mayor of Pinewick,

3
00:00:03,699 --> 00:00:05,838
a small town with one big employer,

4
00:00:05,838 --> 00:00:08,120
the warehouse on the hill.

5
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:08,905
This morning,

6
00:00:08,905 --> 00:00:11,120
the warehouse is throwing a party.

7
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,470
Not for the workers.

8
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:14,010
For the robots.

9
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:15,320
They lift faster.

10
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,670
They never get tired.

11
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:18,480
They never take a sick day.

12
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,643
And there's one more thing robots never

13
00:00:20,643 --> 00:00:21,340
do.

14
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:23,060
They never get a paycheck.

15
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:25,860
Which means they never pay income tax.

16
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:28,224
Rosa looks at the shiny new machines

17
00:00:28,224 --> 00:00:30,228
and asks the question nobody at the

18
00:00:30,228 --> 00:00:31,830
party wants to hear.

19
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:33,786
If robots do the work,

20
00:00:33,786 --> 00:00:35,600
who pays the taxes?

21
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:42,967
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

22
00:00:54,417 --> 00:00:55,354
Meet Rivet,

23
00:00:55,354 --> 00:00:58,047
the warehouse's newest robot.

24
00:00:58,047 --> 00:00:59,315
Rivet is friendly,

25
00:00:59,315 --> 00:00:59,939
polite,

26
00:00:59,939 --> 00:01:00,777
and very,

27
00:01:00,777 --> 00:01:02,667
very good at boxes.

28
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:05,717
Rosa asks Rivet a simple question.

29
00:01:05,787 --> 00:01:07,687
Where does your paycheck go?

30
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:09,117
Rivet checks.

31
00:01:09,117 --> 00:01:10,466
I don't have a paycheck,

32
00:01:10,466 --> 00:01:11,457
it says.

33
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:13,619
I don't even have pockets.

34
00:01:13,884 --> 00:01:16,894
Then Rosa meets someone else.

35
00:01:17,014 --> 00:01:17,664
This is Stub.

36
00:01:17,664 --> 00:01:19,020
Stub is a pay stub,

37
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,089
the last one ever printed for a

38
00:01:21,089 --> 00:01:22,905
forklift driver named Ray,

39
00:01:22,905 --> 00:01:25,454
before his job went to the robots.

40
00:01:25,454 --> 00:01:28,074
Stub has something to show the mayor.

41
00:01:28,444 --> 00:01:29,826
Every paycheck,

42
00:01:29,826 --> 00:01:30,506
he says,

43
00:01:30,506 --> 00:01:33,104
carries a little piece of the town.

44
00:01:33,104 --> 00:01:35,445
Follow me.

45
00:01:35,684 --> 00:01:36,248
Now,

46
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:37,406
to be clear,

47
00:01:37,406 --> 00:01:40,084
Pinewick is a what if.

48
00:01:40,154 --> 00:01:41,604
Across America,

49
00:01:41,604 --> 00:01:43,053
people are still working,

50
00:01:43,053 --> 00:01:45,364
and paychecks are still arriving every

51
00:01:45,364 --> 00:01:46,384
Friday.

52
00:01:46,534 --> 00:01:48,726
Nobody knows how many jobs AI will

53
00:01:48,726 --> 00:01:49,984
really take.

54
00:01:50,034 --> 00:01:51,791
But if it takes a lot of

55
00:01:51,791 --> 00:01:52,208
them,

56
00:01:52,208 --> 00:01:53,723
the problem starts right here,

57
00:01:53,723 --> 00:01:55,700
in the little slip of paper in

58
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:56,959
Rosa's hand.

59
00:02:01,417 --> 00:02:04,297
So Rosa follows Stub.

60
00:02:04,397 --> 00:02:05,698
Every payday,

61
00:02:05,698 --> 00:02:07,882
before Ray ever saw his money,

62
00:02:07,882 --> 00:02:10,247
some of it was already gone.

63
00:02:10,757 --> 00:02:11,910
In most states,

64
00:02:11,910 --> 00:02:14,240
a slice went to the state capital,

65
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,487
to help pay for schools and roads.

66
00:02:16,517 --> 00:02:19,035
A slice went to Washington as income

67
00:02:19,035 --> 00:02:19,857
tax.

68
00:02:19,857 --> 00:02:23,122
And another slice went to Social Security,

69
00:02:23,122 --> 00:02:25,471
the check that Ray's parents get every

70
00:02:25,471 --> 00:02:26,306
month.

71
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:29,220
Then Stub shows Rosa the big picture.

72
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:30,826
In America,

73
00:02:30,826 --> 00:02:33,822
almost nine out of every ten dollars

74
00:02:33,822 --> 00:02:36,817
the federal government collects come from

75
00:02:36,817 --> 00:02:37,606
people,

76
00:02:37,606 --> 00:02:40,329
through their income taxes and the taxes

77
00:02:40,329 --> 00:02:42,835
taken out of their paychecks.

78
00:02:43,084 --> 00:02:44,614
And companies?

79
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:47,352
Corporate income tax brought in only about

80
00:02:47,352 --> 00:02:50,252
seven cents of every federal tax dollar

81
00:02:50,252 --> 00:02:52,414
in the year that just ended.

82
00:02:52,694 --> 00:02:54,914
Rivet's company pays that.

83
00:02:55,054 --> 00:02:57,434
Rivet itself pays nothing.

84
00:02:57,544 --> 00:03:00,221
Rivet looks a little embarrassed.

85
00:03:00,484 --> 00:03:03,364
Let me draw why this matters.

86
00:03:03,394 --> 00:03:05,584
Here's the government's money tank.

87
00:03:05,584 --> 00:03:07,692
Most of the water pours in from

88
00:03:07,692 --> 00:03:08,720
one big tap,

89
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:10,374
people's paychecks.

90
00:03:10,534 --> 00:03:12,563
A thin tap drips in from company

91
00:03:12,563 --> 00:03:13,614
profits.

92
00:03:13,664 --> 00:03:15,443
Now watch what happens if robots take

93
00:03:15,443 --> 00:03:16,834
over the work.

94
00:03:16,844 --> 00:03:19,054
The big tap shrinks.

95
00:03:19,084 --> 00:03:20,711
The thin tap might grow,

96
00:03:20,711 --> 00:03:22,414
if profits grow.

97
00:03:22,434 --> 00:03:24,580
But the whole tank was built around

98
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:25,895
the big one.

99
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:28,970
Social Security would feel it first.

100
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:31,258
It's paid for mainly by one tax,

101
00:03:31,258 --> 00:03:32,506
the payroll tax,

102
00:03:32,506 --> 00:03:34,380
a tax on wages.

103
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:35,576
No wages,

104
00:03:35,576 --> 00:03:37,240
no payroll tax.

105
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:39,610
And it's already under strain.

106
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:41,666
Its trustees say its reserve fund is

107
00:03:41,666 --> 00:03:45,268
projected to run dry in 2034.

108
00:03:45,517 --> 00:03:46,534
After that,

109
00:03:46,534 --> 00:03:49,140
the money still coming in from paychecks

110
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:52,242
would cover only part of every check,

111
00:03:52,242 --> 00:03:54,927
unless Congress changes the law.

112
00:03:55,107 --> 00:03:57,553
Stub does the math on his fingers,

113
00:03:57,553 --> 00:04:00,352
and he doesn't have many fingers.

114
00:04:00,617 --> 00:04:02,477
Is America unusual?

115
00:04:02,477 --> 00:04:03,547
Not really.

116
00:04:03,547 --> 00:04:05,658
Across the world's rich democracies,

117
00:04:05,658 --> 00:04:07,791
about half of all tax money comes

118
00:04:07,791 --> 00:04:10,737
from taxes on work and personal income.

119
00:04:10,877 --> 00:04:12,329
Everywhere you look,

120
00:04:12,329 --> 00:04:14,612
the system leans on the paycheck.

121
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:17,477
And Washington already spends more than it

122
00:04:17,477 --> 00:04:18,169
collects,

123
00:04:18,169 --> 00:04:20,557
a story we told in Who Actually

124
00:04:20,557 --> 00:04:22,590
Owns America's Debt.

125
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,950
Rosa doesn't need the details.

126
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:26,770
She gets the picture.

127
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:28,936
If the biggest tap slows down,

128
00:04:28,936 --> 00:04:31,599
the hole only gets deeper.

129
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:38,460
So Rosa goes looking for answers,

130
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:40,407
and she finds a whole fair of

131
00:04:40,407 --> 00:04:41,250
them.

132
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:42,556
Economists,

133
00:04:42,556 --> 00:04:44,936
think tanks and governments have proposed

134
00:04:44,936 --> 00:04:46,340
all kinds of fixes,

135
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:48,958
and every booth promises to keep the

136
00:04:48,958 --> 00:04:50,510
money flowing.

137
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:52,950
Stub gives her one rule.

138
00:04:53,130 --> 00:04:54,937
For every idea,

139
00:04:54,937 --> 00:04:56,482
find one good reason,

140
00:04:56,482 --> 00:04:57,867
and one catch.

141
00:04:58,117 --> 00:04:59,627
Booth one.

142
00:04:59,627 --> 00:05:01,357
Tax the robots.

143
00:05:01,357 --> 00:05:04,125
The idea got famous in 2017,

144
00:05:04,125 --> 00:05:05,674
when Bill Gates said that if a

145
00:05:05,674 --> 00:05:07,203
robot does a worker's job,

146
00:05:07,203 --> 00:05:08,945
maybe the robot should be taxed at

147
00:05:08,945 --> 00:05:10,707
a similar level.

148
00:05:11,197 --> 00:05:13,690
Rivet takes one step backward.

149
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:15,071
That same year,

150
00:05:15,071 --> 00:05:17,940
South Korea tried something close.

151
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:20,220
It didn't put a tax on robots.

152
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:20,848
Instead,

153
00:05:20,848 --> 00:05:23,012
it trimmed a tax break for companies

154
00:05:23,012 --> 00:05:25,560
that buy automation machines.

155
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:27,905
Headlines called it the first robot tax

156
00:05:27,905 --> 00:05:29,123
anyway.

157
00:05:29,384 --> 00:05:30,744
The good reason?

158
00:05:30,744 --> 00:05:33,011
It keeps money coming in as jobs

159
00:05:33,011 --> 00:05:33,490
go,

160
00:05:33,490 --> 00:05:35,505
and it slows things down so workers

161
00:05:35,505 --> 00:05:37,364
have time to adjust.

162
00:05:37,484 --> 00:05:38,574
The catch?

163
00:05:38,574 --> 00:05:41,024
What exactly counts as a robot?

164
00:05:41,024 --> 00:05:42,264
A dishwasher?

165
00:05:42,264 --> 00:05:43,934
A piece of software?

166
00:05:44,034 --> 00:05:45,813
And tax machines too hard,

167
00:05:45,813 --> 00:05:48,065
and companies may just buy fewer of

168
00:05:48,065 --> 00:05:48,493
them,

169
00:05:48,493 --> 00:05:50,444
or build somewhere else.

170
00:05:51,084 --> 00:05:53,969
Europe's lawmakers looked at a robot tax

171
00:05:53,969 --> 00:05:55,678
and turned it down.

172
00:05:55,917 --> 00:05:57,507
Booth two.

173
00:05:57,507 --> 00:05:59,157
Tax the owners.

174
00:05:59,277 --> 00:06:02,038
If robots make companies more profitable,

175
00:06:02,038 --> 00:06:03,087
tax those profits,

176
00:06:03,087 --> 00:06:05,421
or the money their shareholders make,

177
00:06:05,421 --> 00:06:06,877
a little more.

178
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:09,248
In Who Gets Rich When Robots Do

179
00:06:09,248 --> 00:06:09,930
the Work,

180
00:06:09,930 --> 00:06:12,046
we saw that the gains from machines

181
00:06:12,046 --> 00:06:14,527
tend to flow to whoever owns them.

182
00:06:14,784 --> 00:06:16,144
The good reason?

183
00:06:16,144 --> 00:06:18,219
It follows the money to wherever the

184
00:06:18,219 --> 00:06:19,574
robots send it.

185
00:06:19,804 --> 00:06:20,874
The catch?

186
00:06:20,874 --> 00:06:22,614
Profits can travel.

187
00:06:22,804 --> 00:06:25,056
A big company can book its profits

188
00:06:25,056 --> 00:06:27,308
in a country with lower taxes far

189
00:06:27,308 --> 00:06:29,335
more easily than Ray could move his

190
00:06:29,335 --> 00:06:30,161
job.

191
00:06:30,417 --> 00:06:31,817
Booth three.

192
00:06:31,817 --> 00:06:33,497
Tax the shopping.

193
00:06:33,497 --> 00:06:35,301
Robots don't earn anything,

194
00:06:35,301 --> 00:06:37,957
but somebody buys what they make.

195
00:06:38,257 --> 00:06:40,890
Most rich countries already collect a big

196
00:06:40,890 --> 00:06:42,541
national sales tax,

197
00:06:42,541 --> 00:06:44,817
called a value added tax.

198
00:06:44,917 --> 00:06:47,135
America is the only one in the

199
00:06:47,135 --> 00:06:48,541
club without one.

200
00:06:48,784 --> 00:06:50,444
The good reason?

201
00:06:50,474 --> 00:06:52,384
It's hard to dodge.

202
00:06:52,424 --> 00:06:53,346
Everyone who buys things pays,

203
00:06:53,346 --> 00:06:54,844
wherever the robot happens to live.

204
00:06:54,844 --> 00:06:57,194
The catch?

205
00:06:57,194 --> 00:06:59,503
Families with less money spend almost

206
00:06:59,503 --> 00:07:01,096
everything they earn,

207
00:07:01,096 --> 00:07:03,405
so a shopping tax takes a bigger

208
00:07:03,405 --> 00:07:05,252
bite out of their budget than out

209
00:07:05,252 --> 00:07:07,238
of a rich family's.

210
00:07:07,484 --> 00:07:09,329
Let me draw what all three booths

211
00:07:09,329 --> 00:07:10,934
have in common.

212
00:07:10,934 --> 00:07:11,653
Today,

213
00:07:11,653 --> 00:07:14,484
the tax bill sits mostly on workers.

214
00:07:14,524 --> 00:07:16,700
A robot tax slides it over to

215
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:18,754
whoever buys the machines.

216
00:07:18,754 --> 00:07:20,669
A profit tax slides it to the

217
00:07:20,669 --> 00:07:21,644
owners.

218
00:07:21,644 --> 00:07:23,689
A shopping tax slides it to the

219
00:07:23,689 --> 00:07:24,744
shoppers.

220
00:07:24,844 --> 00:07:27,144
Nobody makes the bill disappear.

221
00:07:27,244 --> 00:07:30,046
Each idea just decides whose shoulders it

222
00:07:30,046 --> 00:07:31,156
lands on.

223
00:07:35,617 --> 00:07:38,799
Then Rosa reaches the strangest booth of

224
00:07:38,799 --> 00:07:39,717
all.

225
00:07:39,857 --> 00:07:41,507
It isn't a new tax.

226
00:07:41,507 --> 00:07:42,997
It's a dividend.

227
00:07:43,107 --> 00:07:44,927
The idea is simple.

228
00:07:45,027 --> 00:07:47,092
If robots and AI make the country

229
00:07:47,092 --> 00:07:47,758
richer,

230
00:07:47,758 --> 00:07:49,364
share a slice of that wealth with

231
00:07:49,364 --> 00:07:50,948
every citizen,

232
00:07:50,948 --> 00:07:54,302
the way a company pays its shareholders.

233
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:57,262
One state already does something like

234
00:07:57,262 --> 00:07:57,778
this,

235
00:07:57,778 --> 00:07:58,920
with oil.

236
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,222
Every year,

237
00:08:00,222 --> 00:08:02,922
Alaska sends a check to nearly every

238
00:08:02,922 --> 00:08:03,800
resident,

239
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:05,825
paid out of a giant investment fund

240
00:08:05,825 --> 00:08:07,550
built on oil money.

241
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:10,186
We told that story in Alaska,

242
00:08:10,186 --> 00:08:11,817
The State That Pays You to Live

243
00:08:11,817 --> 00:08:12,650
There.

244
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:14,280
Rosa wonders.

245
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:16,218
Could a country build a fund like

246
00:08:16,218 --> 00:08:18,029
that out of robots?

247
00:08:18,284 --> 00:08:19,854
The good reason?

248
00:08:19,874 --> 00:08:21,914
Everyone shares in the gains,

249
00:08:21,914 --> 00:08:24,206
even people who don't own a single

250
00:08:24,206 --> 00:08:25,494
share of stock.

251
00:08:25,664 --> 00:08:26,794
The catch?

252
00:08:26,854 --> 00:08:29,117
Alaska's fund was filled with oil the

253
00:08:29,117 --> 00:08:31,074
state already owned.

254
00:08:31,234 --> 00:08:32,873
To fill a robot fund,

255
00:08:32,873 --> 00:08:35,010
a government would first have to own

256
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:36,412
part of the robots,

257
00:08:36,412 --> 00:08:37,724
or tax them.

258
00:08:37,924 --> 00:08:39,467
And when money gets tight,

259
00:08:39,467 --> 00:08:41,481
the size of the check becomes a

260
00:08:41,481 --> 00:08:43,199
political fight.

261
00:08:43,450 --> 00:08:46,300
Some people go one step further.

262
00:08:46,350 --> 00:08:49,037
Just give everyone a basic income,

263
00:08:49,037 --> 00:08:50,870
no strings attached.

264
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,268
So Rosa asks,

265
00:08:52,268 --> 00:08:54,680
has anyone actually tried?

266
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:55,730
Yes.

267
00:08:55,730 --> 00:08:59,190
And the results surprised both sides.

268
00:08:59,450 --> 00:09:00,366
In Stockton,

269
00:09:00,366 --> 00:09:01,516
California,

270
00:09:01,516 --> 00:09:03,394
a pilot sent a small group of

271
00:09:03,394 --> 00:09:05,906
residents $500 a month,

272
00:09:05,906 --> 00:09:07,680
no strings attached.

273
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:10,180
Plenty of critics expected people to stop

274
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:11,180
working.

275
00:09:11,220 --> 00:09:12,007
Instead,

276
00:09:12,007 --> 00:09:13,336
after a year,

277
00:09:13,336 --> 00:09:15,506
more of them had full-time jobs than

278
00:09:15,506 --> 00:09:19,089
in a comparison group that got nothing.

279
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:20,900
Then came a much bigger,

280
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:23,124
carefully controlled study,

281
00:09:23,124 --> 00:09:24,921
run by a research group called

282
00:09:24,921 --> 00:09:26,340
OpenResearch.

283
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:28,625
It sent low-income people a thousand

284
00:09:28,625 --> 00:09:29,745
dollars a month,

285
00:09:29,745 --> 00:09:30,408
for years,

286
00:09:30,408 --> 00:09:32,693
and compared them with people who got

287
00:09:32,693 --> 00:09:34,207
almost nothing.

288
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:35,075
This time,

289
00:09:35,075 --> 00:09:36,993
people worked a little less,

290
00:09:36,993 --> 00:09:39,149
on average about an hour or two

291
00:09:39,149 --> 00:09:40,180
a week.

292
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:41,692
They spent more on everyday needs,

293
00:09:41,692 --> 00:09:42,980
and they had more choices.

294
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:44,990
But the money didn't lead to better

295
00:09:44,990 --> 00:09:45,414
jobs,

296
00:09:45,414 --> 00:09:47,630
and the happiness boost faded.

297
00:09:47,630 --> 00:09:48,061
So,

298
00:09:48,061 --> 00:09:48,946
a cushion,

299
00:09:48,946 --> 00:09:50,227
not a cure.

300
00:09:50,484 --> 00:09:51,774
The good reason?

301
00:09:51,774 --> 00:09:53,828
A floor that nobody falls through,

302
00:09:53,828 --> 00:09:55,994
even if work disappears.

303
00:09:55,994 --> 00:09:56,984
The catch?

304
00:09:56,984 --> 00:09:59,467
Paying everyone is enormously expensive,

305
00:09:59,467 --> 00:10:01,007
and that money has to come from

306
00:10:01,007 --> 00:10:02,184
somewhere.

307
00:10:02,234 --> 00:10:04,288
Which brings Rosa right back to her

308
00:10:04,288 --> 00:10:05,659
first question.

309
00:10:10,117 --> 00:10:12,531
Let me draw the whole puzzle on

310
00:10:12,531 --> 00:10:13,657
one page.

311
00:10:13,657 --> 00:10:14,639
On the left,

312
00:10:14,639 --> 00:10:16,377
the things we'll still need.

313
00:10:16,377 --> 00:10:16,992
Schools,

314
00:10:16,992 --> 00:10:17,608
roads,

315
00:10:17,608 --> 00:10:19,827
checks for retirees.

316
00:10:19,877 --> 00:10:20,898
On the right,

317
00:10:20,898 --> 00:10:23,255
the places where AI's wealth will pile

318
00:10:23,255 --> 00:10:23,977
up.

319
00:10:23,977 --> 00:10:24,596
Profits,

320
00:10:24,596 --> 00:10:25,214
shopping,

321
00:10:25,214 --> 00:10:27,287
and the robots themselves.

322
00:10:27,287 --> 00:10:29,998
Every proposal is just a different pipe

323
00:10:29,998 --> 00:10:32,057
from the right side to the left.

324
00:10:32,067 --> 00:10:34,104
The real question isn't whether to build

325
00:10:34,104 --> 00:10:34,987
a pipe.

326
00:10:34,987 --> 00:10:35,991
It's which one,

327
00:10:35,991 --> 00:10:38,392
and who's standing under it.

328
00:10:38,650 --> 00:10:39,157
So,

329
00:10:39,157 --> 00:10:41,266
if AI replaces workers,

330
00:10:41,266 --> 00:10:43,150
who pays the taxes?

331
00:10:43,150 --> 00:10:44,484
The honest answer is,

332
00:10:44,484 --> 00:10:46,550
nobody has decided yet.

333
00:10:46,550 --> 00:10:47,185
Today,

334
00:10:47,185 --> 00:10:48,850
it's mostly workers.

335
00:10:48,850 --> 00:10:49,727
If work shrinks,

336
00:10:49,727 --> 00:10:51,054
the bill has to move,

337
00:10:51,054 --> 00:10:51,931
to profits,

338
00:10:51,931 --> 00:10:52,809
to shopping,

339
00:10:52,809 --> 00:10:54,810
to the owners of the robots,

340
00:10:54,810 --> 00:10:56,760
or to some mix of them.

341
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:59,041
Every option has a good reason and

342
00:10:59,041 --> 00:11:00,030
a catch.

343
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:01,129
And in the end,

344
00:11:01,129 --> 00:11:03,550
the choice isn't really about robots.

345
00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:06,333
It's about which people carry the bill.

346
00:11:06,584 --> 00:11:08,744
And there's a clock ticking.

347
00:11:08,774 --> 00:11:11,691
Social Security's reserve is projected to

348
00:11:11,691 --> 00:11:14,002
run dry in 2034,

349
00:11:14,002 --> 00:11:16,021
long before robots could take most of

350
00:11:16,021 --> 00:11:16,672
the jobs,

351
00:11:16,672 --> 00:11:18,394
if they ever do.

352
00:11:18,724 --> 00:11:21,210
So this debate may well arrive before

353
00:11:21,210 --> 00:11:22,805
the robots do.

354
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:24,587
Back in Pinewick,

355
00:11:24,587 --> 00:11:26,980
Rosa calls a town meeting.

356
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:28,019
She brings Stub,

357
00:11:28,019 --> 00:11:29,950
and she brings Rivet.

358
00:11:29,950 --> 00:11:31,423
Here's what we'll still need,

359
00:11:31,423 --> 00:11:32,310
she says.

360
00:11:32,310 --> 00:11:34,470
And here's who could pay for it.

361
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:36,072
Let's decide together,

362
00:11:36,072 --> 00:11:38,540
while the paychecks are still flowing.

363
00:11:38,690 --> 00:11:40,820
Rivet raises a hand.

364
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:42,370
Do I get a vote?

365
00:11:42,430 --> 00:11:43,064
Not yet,

366
00:11:43,064 --> 00:11:44,200
says Rosa.

367
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:46,231
But you might get a bill.

368
00:11:46,484 --> 00:11:47,153
Next time,

369
00:11:47,153 --> 00:11:50,150
we follow the money behind another hidden

370
00:11:50,150 --> 00:11:52,594
link of the world economy.

371
00:11:52,744 --> 00:11:55,271
This is The Hidden Economics.
